entries.doc:
Mixing moves: what three.js's AnimationMixer does for meshes, for our
skeletons. A move here is a function of time that poses a skeleton (as the
zoo's moves and the elf's walk are), so a mix poses the skeleton once per
move and blends the results.

- `takePose` / `putPose` copy a pose out and back;
- `blendPose` moves the pose now part of the way toward or away from another
  (rotations by the short way, offsets and sizes straight);
- `addPose` lays a move on top as a change from a reference pose, so a nod
  or a breath can ride on any walk;
- `Mixer` plays named moves with weights and crossfades between them.

The rest blobs with the morphs applied, into `out` (as many blobs as `rest`;
colors and opacities are left alone). 'centers' moves the centers only, as a
mesh morph would; 'shape' also pushes each blob's shape through J.

props.doc:
A rim of light: a scene pixel whose neighbor toward the light (this way across the picture, x right and y down;
`rimStep`) is backdrop takes the palette color a step lighter in its own hue (`lighterStep`), as a pixel artist
picks out the lit edge of a form.

modules.doc:
Watercolor (Matt picked it, 2026-11-19): a scene painted in thin washes on paper. Paint is see-through, so a wash
darkens the paper under it and never covers it; its edges run a little and dry darker where the pigment pools;
the paper's grain shows through; the lightest places are left as bare paper. The arithmetic here is pure;
`splat-engine/washPass.ts` is its shader twin (change both).
